BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar Livewhale": "https://calendar.santarosa.edu/event/23769-standing-in-her-power-portraits-beyond-the-colonial-fr"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_livewhale
SUMMARY:Standing in Her Power: Portraits Beyond the Colonial Frame
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261117
DTSTAMP:20260825T133156Z
UID:20261116T080000Z-23769@calendar.santarosa.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261116
ATTACH:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/live/image/gid/3/width/80/height/80/
 crop/1/src_region/0,0,1545,1545/718_cs_Standing-in-her-power-flyer-8.5X11.
 rev.1789492221.jpg
CATEGORIES:Museum
DESCRIPTION:Spanning ages 20 to 81\, the women featured in the exhibition 
 represent a diversity of lives\, identities and experiences that challenge
  any single representation of Indigenous womanhood. They are land defender
 s\, language keepers\, dancers\, bead workers\, mothers\, daughters and ar
 tists\, each with their own relationship to culture\, community\, land and
  identity.  Julian Cook\, an SRJC anthropology student and co-creator of t
 he project\, conceived the exhibition shortly after she began working at t
 he SRJC Multicultural Museum. While cataloging the museum's book collectio
 n\, Cook came across Edward S. Curtis: The Women\, a collection of photogr
 aphs that\, despite being shaped by romanticized notions of Native peoples
  as a "vanishing race\," also captured the dignity and everyday lives of N
 ative women.  "I don't want to replace one narrow image of Native womanhoo
 d with another\," Cook explains. "I want to challenge the idea that there 
 is any single look of a Native woman\, and to give these women the opportu
 nity to decide for themselves how they wanted to be seen."  To bring that 
 vision to life\, the museum collaborated with San Francisco-based Mexican 
 American photographer Alexa "LexMex" Treviño (https://www.lexmexart.com/a
 bout)\, whose work explores cultural identity\, community and representati
 on through portraiture. Her previous projects include Corazón de la Misi
 ón\, a series of monumental public portraits honoring members of San Fran
 cisco's Latinx community.  Each woman featured in Standing in Her Power ex
 ercised complete creative control over her portrait\, choosing how to pres
 ent herself rather than conforming to someone else's expectations of what 
 a Native woman should look like. The accompanying stories offer a more per
 sonal perspective on the connections between identity\, culture\, ancestry
 \, and community.This project is funded by a grant from the Santa Rosa Jun
 ior College Foundation Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endowment.  For
  more information\, visit the SRJC Multicultural Museum website (https://m
 useum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-her-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-fra
 me).
LAST-MODIFIED:20261112T184143Z
LOCATION:Multicultural Museum - Santa Rosa Campus
URL:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/event/23769-standing-in-her-power-portr
 aits-beyond-the-colonial-fr
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Spanning ages 20 to 81\, the women featu
 red in the exhibition represent a diversity of lives\, identities and expe
 riences that challenge any single representation of Indigenous womanhood. 
 They are land defenders\, language keepers\, dancers\, bead workers\, moth
 ers\, daughters and artists\, each with their own relationship to culture\
 , community\, land and identity.</p><p>  Julian Cook\, an SRJC anthropolog
 y student and co-creator of the project\, conceived the exhibition shortly
  after she began working at the SRJC Multicultural Museum. While catalogin
 g the museum's book collection\, Cook came across <em>Edward S. Curtis: Th
 e Women</em>\, a collection of photographs that\, despite being shaped by 
 romanticized notions of Native peoples as a "vanishing race\," also captur
 ed the dignity and everyday lives of Native women.</p><p>  "I don't want t
 o replace one narrow image of Native womanhood with another\," Cook explai
 ns. "I want to challenge the idea that there is any single look of a Nativ
 e woman\, and to give these women the opportunity to decide for themselves
  how they wanted to be seen."</p><p>  To bring that vision to life\, the m
 useum collaborated with San Francisco-based Mexican American photographer 
 <a href="https://www.lexmexart.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A
 lexa "LexMex" Treviño</a>\, whose work explores cultural identity\, commu
 nity and representation through portraiture. Her previous projects include
  <em>Corazón de la Misión</em>\, a series of monumental public portraits
  honoring members of San Francisco's Latinx community.</p><p>  Each woman 
 featured in <em>Standing in Her Power</em> exercised complete creative con
 trol over her portrait\, choosing how to present herself rather than confo
 rming to someone else's expectations of what a Native woman should look li
 ke. The accompanying stories offer a more personal perspective on the conn
 ections between identity\, culture\, ancestry\, and community.</p><p><em>T
 his project is funded by a grant from the Santa Rosa Junior College Founda
 tion Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endowment.</em></p><p>  For more 
 information\, visit the <a href="https://museum.santarosa.edu/event/standi
 ng-her-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-frame" target="_blank" rel="noopene
 r">SRJC Multicultural Museum website</a>.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ALL-DAY:1
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:23769
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/live/image/gid/3/width/80
 /height/80/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1545\,1545/718_cs_Standing-in-her-power
 -flyer-8.5X11.rev.1789492221.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/Los_Angeles
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:Santa Rosa Junior College
END:VEVENT
