BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Sebastopolcalendar": "https://sebastopolcalendar.com/event/art-salon-the-future-of-culture-the-role-and-purpose-of-art/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:sebastopolcalendar
X-SOURCE:Sebastopol Calendar
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T190000
DTSTAMP:20261111T160156Z
CREATED:20261111T160156Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261111T160156Z
UID:10009166-1795194000-1795201200@sebastopolcalendar.com
SUMMARY:Art Salon | The Future of Culture\, & the Role and Purpose of Art
DESCRIPTION:Apocalypse Studio will be hosting a Parisian-style art salon on the last night of its major solo art exhibition\, STIMULANT | Art as Remedy for Diseased Culture at Laguna Lab. \nCome to see the exhibition of large physical paintings and digital artworks\, with the Oversoul as the centerpiece — a 144 piece mosaic 10 feet x 15 feet wide of painted panels with digital artwork counterparts. We will give some presentations about the exhibition\, introduce the Apocalypse Studio and share its mission and projects for 2027\, and hold a lightly facilitated conversation about the Future of culture and the role and purpose of art. A light dinner with drinks will be served. Vegetarian options will be available. RSVP by Nov 18; there is limited seating. Please email song@apocalypse.art to RSVP or with questions.
URL:https://sebastopolcalendar.com/event/art-salon-the-future-of-culture-the-role-and-purpose-of-art/
LOCATION:Laguna Lab\, 117 Morris St\, Sebastopol\, 95472
CATEGORIES:Adults and teens
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://sebastopolcalendar.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/art-salon-1200x800-1.jpg
ORGANIZER;CN="Apocalypse Studio":MAILTO:song@apocalypse.art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"North Bay Bohemian": "https://bit.ly/JakeSloanVallejoMuseumEvent"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"North Bay Bohemian": "https://vallejomuseum.net/event/standing-tall-the-story-of-the-mare-island-21ers/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:bohemian
SUMMARY:Standing Tall: The Story of the Mare Island 21ers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T210000
UID:261110g-JBW5mji0yZ2kjJodW8wQ
DESCRIPTION:On November 17\, 1961\, twenty-one Black workers at the Mare I
 sland Naval Shipyard did something few people dared: They filed a formal c
 omplaint of racial discrimination against the United States government.\n\
 nThey were pipefitters\, mechanics\, welders\, painters\, and laborers who
  had endured years of unequal treatment in hiring\, training\, promotions\
 , and pay. They filed their complaint before the Civil Rights Act of 1964 
 and before the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission existed. They put t
 heir jobs on the line anyway. Represented by attorney Charles Wilson\, the
 y pressed their case against the federal government at a time when few wou
 ld\, and their stand helped build the pressure that changed federal employ
 ment law.\n\nSixty-five years later\, the Vallejo Naval & Historical Museu
 m marks the anniversary with an evening of living history\, right here in 
 the city where it happened. Opening Remarks\n\nScott Stick\, Audio Visual 
 Archivist for the Vallejo Naval & Historical Museum\, will open the evenin
 g.\n\nModerator: Brendan Riley\, Vallejo historian and longtime Associated
  Press reporter Panelists\n\nJake Sloan\, the youngest of the Original 21e
 rs and author of Standing Tall: Willie Long vs. U.S. Government at Mare Is
 land Naval Shipyard. Pastor Anthony Gilmore\, who took the initiative to t
 alk with his uncle\, Original 21er Clarence Williams\, then brought that s
 tory to Matthias Gafni at the Vallejo Times-Herald. Matthias Gafni\, now a
  reporter for the San Francisco Chronicle\, wrote a detailed three-part se
 ries on the 21ers while at the Vallejo Times-Herald.\n\nWilhelmenia Wilson
  – Daughter of Charles Wilson\, the attorney for the 21ers. She is curre
 ntly the Executive Director of Healthy Black Families\, Inc. \\(www.health
 yblackfam.org\\)\n\nSharon McGriff-Payne\, a local historian who will spea
 k to the early African American experience in Solano County\, leading up t
 o and including Mare Island Naval Shipyard.\n\nTogether\, they'll trace ho
 w a story nearly lost to history was passed from one generation to the nex
 t\, from a nephew&\\#39\;s conversation with his uncle\, one of the origin
 al 21\, to the pages of a local newspaper\, to a book written by a man who
  was there. Schedule\n\n5:30 PM: Doors open 6:00 PM: Panel discussion 7:30
  PM: Q&A and book signing\n\nAdmission: Free\n\nSuggested donation of $25 
 per person\n\nSeating is limited\, so please RSVP to reserve your spot. Co
 pies of Standing Tall will be available for purchase.
LOCATION:Vallejo Naval and Historical Museum
URL:https://vallejomuseum.net/event/standing-tall-the-story-of-the-mare-is
 land-21ers/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
