X-SOURCE-ID:meetup_novel_lovers_of_sebastopol
X-SOURCE:Novel Lovers of Sonoma County
UID:event_317107531@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20261114T061706Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270130T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270130T143000
SUMMARY:John of John by Douglas Stuart (option 2)
DESCRIPTION:Novel Lovers of Sonoma County\, CA\n*Publisher's description*:
  A vivid\, moving novel following a young man returning to his Hebridean i
 sland home\, a portrait of a father's expectations and a son's desires.\n\
 nOut of money and with little to show for his art school education\, John-
 Calum Macleod takes the ferry back home to the Isle of Harris in the Outer
  Hebrides to find that little has changed except for him. He returns to th
 e windswept croft and the two pillars of his childhood: his father John\, 
 a sheep farmer\, tweed weaver\, and lay preacher in the local Presbyterian
  church\, and his maternal grandmother Ella\, a profanity-loving Glaswegia
 n whose steady warmth helped Cal weather the sudden departure of his mothe
 r.\n\nCal privately wonders if any lonely men might be found on the barren
  hillsides of home\, while John is dismayed by his son's long hair\, stran
 ge clothes\, and seeming unwillingness to be Saved. But Cal isn't the only
  one who is keeping secrets. As lambing season turns to shearing season\, 
 the threads holding together the community together become increasingly fr
 ayed\, and nothing will remain as it was before. *John of John* is a singu
 lar novel about duty\, passion\, and the transformative power of the truth
 .\n\n*From our members*:\n\nMac: "a must-read for our Novel Lovers discuss
 ions. It contains beautiful\, accurate descriptions of the harsh sere land
 scape in the Outer Hebrides where I’ve visited. It’s filled with secre
 ts\, many revealed and others not. Stuart dishes up human hope and despair
  in equal measure. Having dwelt on very different islands with small face-
 to-face communities like those on Harris and Lewis\, the novel reflects li
 fe in many other places besides the far west of Scotland."\n\nMatthew: "I 
 did appreciate *John of John* and greatly admire Douglas Stuart's thoughtf
 ul writing. Sheep farming on a remote Scottish isle is a world so removed.
  It's very much an internal dialogue with the handful of characters\, with
  a number ancillary family members and neighbors. I finished the book seve
 ral weeks ago and it's stayed with me."\n\nMa'ayan: "I LOVED it all the wa
 y through. It broke my heart but also gave me hope. I laughed out loud at 
 certain parts\, too. The writing is so visceral. This one really stole my 
 heart."\n\nJoan: "So much in this book. It's about finding/denying/finding
  one's true self\; keeping family secrets and secrets kept from family\; p
 assion and denial of passion\; staying upright when family\, religion\, an
 d the hardscrabble life of sheep farming on a Scottish island beat you dow
 n. Some books just get to you. This one gets to me."
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/novel-lovers-of-sebastopol/events/317
 107531/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20261023T193644Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261023T193644Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
